    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Business 2023 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "bus-2023-hl-s3-q7bi", topicId: "business-3-18", conceptId: "paye-calculation-with-tax-credits",
    section: "3", questionRef: "2023 HL Section 3 Q7(B)(i)",
    stem: "Centre Parcs: Centre Parcs Longford features 400 acres of beautiful forest, swimming pool, a range of restaurants, activities and accommodation types. Read the information supplied and answer the questions which follow. Tommy is a manager at Centre Parcs Ireland and earns a gross annual salary of €85,000. His employer provides him with a company car worth €25,000. This is treated as income for tax purposes and is taxed accordingly. Tommy’s tax information is shown below.",
    questionText: "Calculate each of the following: (i) The PAYE which Tommy has to pay",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"10 marks","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Gross Salary 85,000 Benefit in Kind 25,000 Taxable Income 110,000", marks: 2, contextNote: "The examiner's circled 2 sits on the Taxable Income line: the company car is added to the salary before any band, credit or levy is applied, and this same 110,000 is what (iii) charges PRSI on." },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "40,000 x 0.20 8,000", marks: 3, contextNote: "The circled 3 here is the largest single award in this part, and it sits on the 8,000. Neither figure is printed in bold: €40,000 is the fixed standard-rate band given on the paper, so 8,000 is 8,000 whatever your taxable income came to, and there is no own-figures fallback on this line." },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "70,000 x 0.40 28,000", marks: 2, contextNote: "Two separate circled 1s here, one on the 70,000 balance and one on the 28,000. The scheme prints both in bold and adds 'Own figures are shown in bold.', so the marks stand where the 40% is applied correctly to your own balance, even if your taxable income was wrong." },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "Gross PAYE 36,000 Less Tax Credits: Single Person 1,700 PAYE 1,700 Dependent Relative 245 (3645)", marks: 2, contextNote: "Two separate circled 1s again, one on Gross PAYE 36,000 and one on the (3645) credits total. The scheme sets those two figures in the same column of its table, one above the other: they are the two lines of a single subtraction whose answer, €32,355, is boxed in the column beside them. 36,000 is printed in bold — 'Own figures are shown in bold.' — so it stands on your own band figures, but the three credits are fixed amounts given on the paper and are not bold, so 3,645 is the only credits total that scores. Both lines are needed for this row, and the examiner ticks them separately: a candidate who had the gross PAYE but dropped the €245 Dependent Relative credit still keeps 1 of these 2 marks." },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "PAYE Tommy has to Pay €32,355", marks: 1, contextNote: "€32,355 is boxed and printed in bold, and the scheme states 'Own figures are shown in bold.', so this mark is available where Gross PAYE less credits is subtracted correctly on your own figures." },
    ],
