    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Business 2016 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "bus-2016-hl-s3-q6c", topicId: "business-5-14", conceptId: "review-costs-medron-plc-decreased-its-variable-costs-per",
    section: "3", questionRef: "2016 HL Section 3 Q6(c)",
    questionText: "Following a review of costs, Medron plc decreased its variable costs per unit to €10. (c)(i) Calculate the new breakeven point and illustrate on your breakeven chart the new total cost line (TC2) and the new breakeven point (BE2). (c)(ii) Outline one limitation of a breakeven analysis when making business decisions. (20 marks) (60 marks)",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 60,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "(ii) Outline one limitation of a breakeven analysis Break-even analysis assumes Fixed Costs are constant – FCs can vary as output changes It assumes that the business knows all its costs and can break them down into fixed and variable Variable costs may increase/decrease as output increases – business may be able to purchase larger amounts of raw materials at lower prices", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "(economies of scale from bulk buying) Assumes that firm sells all its output – in times of low demand a firm may have difficulty in selling all its products Assumes a firm sells all its output at a single price -firms may offer discounts for bulk purchases/ may produce more than it can sell and may have to sell the surplus at a discounted price Ignores the effect that a change of price may have on sales-price increase", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "fall in demand for the product and vice versa Most businesses sell more than one product, so break-even becomes harder to calculate", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "per unit would decrease to €10. Calculate the new Break-Even", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "point and illustrate how this decrease in variable costs will affect", marks: null },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "the total cost line and the original break-even point. BEP2", marks: null },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "Calculation", marks: null },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "BEP = Fixed Costs /Contribution = 400,000/30-10 = 20,000 units Illustration", marks: null },
    { id: "r-9", kind: "point", verbatim: "BEP2 =", marks: null },
    { id: "r-10", kind: "point", verbatim: "Units Selling Variable Fixed Total Costs Total Profit/loss", marks: null },
    { id: "r-11", kind: "point", verbatim: "Price Costs Costs Revenue", marks: null },
    { id: "r-12", kind: "point", verbatim: "(ii) Outline one limitation of a breakeven analysis", marks: null },
    ],
